A `catch` block contains statements that specify what to do if an exception is thrown in the `try` block. If any statement within
the `try` block (or in a function called from within the `try` block) throws an exception, control is immediately shifted to the
`catch` block. If no exception is thrown in the `try` block, the `catch` block is skipped.
The `finally` block will always execute before control flow exits the `try...catch...finally` construct. It always executes, regardless
of whether an exception was thrown or caught.
