A new Iterator object that yields the values from each of the input iterables in sequence.
## Description
The `Iterator.concat()` method is conceptually similar to `Array`'s
[`concat()`](/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/concat) method, but it operates on any kind of
iterable, and returns an iterator instead of an array. This means that the iterables can be lazily iterated, avoiding unnecessary
allocation or computation. It also means that, technically, you can [concatenate _infinite
iterables_](#concatenating_infinite_iterables), but results from iterables after the first infinite iterable will never be reached.
While each iterable can be infinite, the list of iterables must be finite -- and quite limited in number because engines impose a
very low limit on the number of function arguments. If you need to concatenate a large -- even infinite -- number of iterables,
