even within the same locale  --  output variations are by design and allowed by the specification. It may also not be what you
expect. For example, the string may use non-breaking spaces or be surrounded by bidirectional control characters. You should
not compare the results of `format()` to hardcoded constants.
## Examples
### Basic format usage
The following example shows how to create a relative time formatter using the English language.
// Create a relative time formatter in your locale
// with default values explicitly passed in.
const rtf = new Intl.RelativeTimeFormat("en", {
  localeMatcher: "best fit", // other values: "lookup"
  numeric: "always", // other values: "auto"
  style: "long", // other values: "short" or "narrow"
});
// Format relative time using negative value (-1).
rtf.format(-1, "day"); // "1 day ago"
// Format relative time using positive value (1).
rtf.format(1, "day"); // "in 1 day"
