(Think of the initializer expression as being implicitly wrapped in a function.)
Because instance fields of a class are added before the respective constructor runs, you can access the fields' values within
the constructor. However, because instance fields of a derived class are defined after `super()` returns, the base class's
constructor does not have access to the derived class's fields.
class Base {
  constructor() {
    console.log("Base constructor:", this.field);
