  - : This is `true` if the last value in the sequence has already been consumed. If `value` is present alongside `done`, it is the
iterator's return value.
Once created, an iterator object can be iterated explicitly by repeatedly calling `next()`. Iterating over an iterator is said to
consume the iterator, because it is generally only possible to do once. After a terminating value has been yielded additional
calls to `next()` should continue to return `{done: true}`.
The most common iterator in JavaScript is the Array iterator, which returns each value in the associated array in sequence.
While it is easy to imagine that all iterators could be expressed as arrays, this is not true. Arrays must be allocated in their
